1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:08,690
If you had started driving to Neptune when the Great Pyramid was being built, you would still be on the road today, with about four hundred years left to drive.

2
00:00:09,390 --> 00:00:13,205
Today we're buying tickets to every planet in the solar system.

3
00:00:13,205 --> 00:00:14,476
Three ways to travel.

4
00:00:14,476 --> 00:00:15,687
One departure board.

5
00:00:15,687 --> 00:00:19,502
And one destination that is much harder to reach than it looks.

6
00:00:20,202 --> 00:00:22,075
Before we start, make a guess.

7
00:00:22,075 --> 00:00:25,072
Driving nonstop to Mars, how long would it take?

8
00:00:25,072 --> 00:00:25,571
A month?

9
00:00:25,571 --> 00:00:26,008
A year?

10
00:00:26,008 --> 00:00:26,695
A lifetime?

11
00:00:26,695 --> 00:00:30,378
Keep your guess in mind, because we reach Mars at gate two.

12
00:00:31,078 --> 00:00:33,596
How Long Would It Take to Travel to Every Planet?

13
00:00:34,296 --> 00:00:35,379
First, the rules.

14
00:00:35,379 --> 00:00:40,349
Vehicle one is a car, driving at a hundred kilometers per hour, with no stops.

15
00:00:40,349 --> 00:00:42,770
No sleep, no gas stations, no traffic.

16
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:47,485
Vehicle two is a passenger jet, at about nine hundred kilometers per hour.

17
00:00:47,485 --> 00:00:58,253
Vehicle three is the fastest machine humans have ever built, NASA's Parker Solar Probe, at its record speed of about six hundred ninety two thousand kilometers per hour.

18
00:00:58,953 --> 00:01:00,270
And we'll be generous.

19
00:01:00,270 --> 00:01:06,559
We'll leave on the day each planet comes closest to Earth, and we'll travel in a perfectly straight line.

20
00:01:06,559 --> 00:01:10,152
Real spacecraft can't do that, and we'll see why at the end.

21
00:01:10,152 --> 00:01:12,009
For now, the doors are closing.

22
00:01:12,709 --> 00:01:13,400
Gate zero.

23
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:14,092
A warm-up.

24
00:01:14,092 --> 00:01:14,714
The Moon.

25
00:01:15,414 --> 00:01:19,656
The Moon is about three hundred eighty four thousand kilometers away.

26
00:01:19,656 --> 00:01:24,206
By car, that's a hundred sixty days of nonstop driving, about five months.

27
00:01:24,206 --> 00:01:25,558
By jet, eighteen days.

28
00:01:25,558 --> 00:01:28,448
If you walked, it would take almost nine years.

29
00:01:28,448 --> 00:01:31,829
Our fastest probe would be there in about half an hour.

30
00:01:31,829 --> 00:01:35,702
The Apollo astronauts, for comparison, needed about three days.

31
00:01:36,402 --> 00:01:36,989
Gate one.

32
00:01:36,989 --> 00:01:39,794
Venus, the planet that comes closest to us.

33
00:01:40,494 --> 00:01:44,757
At its closest, Venus is about thirty eight million kilometers away.

34
00:01:44,757 --> 00:01:47,578
That's a hundred times farther than the Moon.

35
00:01:47,578 --> 00:01:50,336
By car, forty four years of nonstop driving.

36
00:01:50,336 --> 00:01:52,718
Leave at twenty, arrive at sixty four.

37
00:01:52,718 --> 00:01:54,347
By jet, almost five years.

38
00:01:54,347 --> 00:01:56,667
Parker would need just over two days.

39
00:01:56,667 --> 00:01:58,297
The real record is slower.

40
00:01:58,297 --> 00:02:03,876
The European probe Venus Express took about five months to get there and slip into orbit.

41
00:02:04,575 --> 00:02:05,494
Gate two.

42
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:06,005
Mars.

43
00:02:06,705 --> 00:02:10,953
Mars at its closest is about fifty five million kilometers away.

44
00:02:10,953 --> 00:02:12,546
By car, sixty two years.

45
00:02:12,546 --> 00:02:16,263
Most people who start that drive would not live to park.

46
00:02:16,263 --> 00:02:17,989
By jet, about seven years.

47
00:02:17,989 --> 00:02:19,649
Parker, about three days.

48
00:02:19,649 --> 00:02:25,158
But NASA's Perseverance rover took two hundred and three days, almost seven months.

49
00:02:25,858 --> 00:02:26,601
Why so slow?

50
00:02:26,601 --> 00:02:29,509
Because a spacecraft doesn't drive toward Mars.

51
00:02:29,509 --> 00:02:36,255
It's thrown into a long curve around the Sun, timed so that Mars arrives at the same spot at the same moment.

52
00:02:36,255 --> 00:02:40,834
Launch a few weeks late, and you wait about two years for the next window.

53
00:02:41,534 --> 00:02:42,897
And the curve is long.

54
00:02:42,897 --> 00:02:46,367
Perseverance didn't cover fifty five million kilometers.

55
00:02:46,367 --> 00:02:55,227
By NASA's count, it traveled about four hundred seventy two million kilometers to reach Mars, more than eight times the straight-line distance.

56
00:02:55,227 --> 00:03:00,307
In space, the shortest path in time is almost never the shortest path in distance.

57
00:03:01,007 --> 00:03:01,654
Gate three.

58
00:03:01,654 --> 00:03:02,124
Mercury.

59
00:03:02,124 --> 00:03:03,887
This is where it gets strange.

60
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:08,758
Mercury is closer to us than Jupiter by a factor of almost eight.

61
00:03:08,758 --> 00:03:10,811
By car, it's eighty eight years.

62
00:03:10,811 --> 00:03:11,580
By jet, ten.

63
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:13,569
So you'd expect it to be quick.

64
00:03:13,569 --> 00:03:14,147
It isn't.

65
00:03:14,147 --> 00:03:19,536
NASA's MESSENGER probe needed six and a half years to get into orbit around Mercury.

66
00:03:19,536 --> 00:03:22,230
Longer than it took Juno to reach Jupiter.

67
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:23,921
Here's the catch.

68
00:03:23,921 --> 00:03:29,810
Earth is racing around the Sun at about thirty kilometers per second, and so is everything we launch.

69
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:34,767
To reach Mercury, you're falling toward the Sun, and you speed up the whole way down.

70
00:03:34,767 --> 00:03:37,332
Arrive like that, and you fly straight past.

71
00:03:37,332 --> 00:03:45,437
So MESSENGER spent years braking, swinging past Earth once, Venus twice and Mercury three times, using each planet's gravity to shed speed.

72
00:03:46,137 --> 00:03:47,672
And it's still happening.

73
00:03:47,672 --> 00:03:52,215
The European and Japanese mission BepiColombo launched in twenty eighteen.

74
00:03:52,215 --> 00:03:57,556
It's due to slip into orbit around Mercury this month, eight years after leaving Earth.

75
00:03:57,556 --> 00:04:02,713
Nine flybys, for a planet that, on average, is actually the closest planet to Earth.

76
00:04:03,413 --> 00:04:04,461
Yes, on average.

77
00:04:04,461 --> 00:04:09,835
Venus comes closest, but it also spends long stretches on the far side of the Sun.

78
00:04:09,835 --> 00:04:14,946
Mercury never strays far from the Sun, so averaged over time, it stays nearer.

79
00:04:14,946 --> 00:04:23,596
In fact, according to a twenty nineteen analysis in Physics Today, Mercury is the closest planet, on average, to every other planet.

80
00:04:23,596 --> 00:04:24,447
Even Neptune.

81
00:04:25,147 --> 00:04:25,776
Gate four.

82
00:04:25,776 --> 00:04:26,280
Jupiter.

83
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:28,795
Now the board starts to look ridiculous.

84
00:04:29,495 --> 00:04:33,251
Five hundred eighty eight million kilometers at its closest.

85
00:04:33,251 --> 00:04:35,629
By car, six hundred seventy one years.

86
00:04:35,629 --> 00:04:39,510
A family would need about twenty generations behind the wheel.

87
00:04:39,510 --> 00:04:42,452
By jet, seventy five years, a whole human life.

88
00:04:42,452 --> 00:04:44,017
Parker, thirty five days.

89
00:04:44,017 --> 00:04:49,399
Juno did it in just under five years, with an extra swing past Earth to pick up speed.

90
00:04:50,099 --> 00:04:50,833
Gate five.

91
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:51,347
Saturn.

92
00:04:51,347 --> 00:04:53,769
One point two billion kilometers.

93
00:04:54,469 --> 00:04:57,261
By car, about thirteen hundred and sixty years.

94
00:04:57,261 --> 00:05:01,835
If you had left in the year six hundred sixty, you'd be pulling up about now.

95
00:05:01,835 --> 00:05:03,855
By jet, a hundred and fifty years.

96
00:05:03,855 --> 00:05:10,627
The Cassini spacecraft took almost seven years, bouncing off Venus twice, Earth once, and Jupiter once on the way.

97
00:05:10,627 --> 00:05:13,775
Even light needs more than an hour to make that trip.

98
00:05:14,475 --> 00:05:15,835
Gates six and seven.

99
00:05:15,835 --> 00:05:17,127
Uranus and Neptune.

100
00:05:17,127 --> 00:05:20,662
Only one spacecraft has ever visited either of them.

101
00:05:21,362 --> 00:05:26,708
Uranus is two point six billion kilometers away, nearly three thousand years by car.

102
00:05:26,708 --> 00:05:35,174
Neptune, at four point three billion kilometers, is almost five thousand years by car, and about five hundred and fifty years by jet.

103
00:05:35,174 --> 00:05:40,583
Even our record-breaking Parker Solar Probe would need about eight and a half months.

104
00:05:41,283 --> 00:05:44,074
The one spacecraft that made it is Voyager two.

105
00:05:44,074 --> 00:05:48,823
It reached Uranus eight and a half years after launch, and Neptune after twelve.

106
00:05:48,823 --> 00:05:59,033
It only managed it because the giant planets had lined up in a pattern that happens about once every hundred and seventy five years, letting it bounce from one to the next.

107
00:05:59,733 --> 00:06:02,185
Out there, even a conversation is slow.

108
00:06:02,185 --> 00:06:08,033
When Voyager two flew past Neptune, its radio signals needed about four hours to reach Earth.

109
00:06:08,033 --> 00:06:13,378
The engineers were watching pictures of a moment that had already ended hours before.

110
00:06:13,378 --> 00:06:17,717
The flyby was programmed in advance, and the spacecraft did it alone.

111
00:06:18,417 --> 00:06:19,875
Here's the whole board.

112
00:06:19,875 --> 00:06:23,553
For the car, every planet is a trip measured in lifetimes.

113
00:06:23,553 --> 00:06:27,421
For the jet, everything past Mars takes longer than a career.

114
00:06:27,421 --> 00:06:32,241
And for light, the fastest thing there is, Neptune is about four hours away.

115
00:06:32,941 --> 00:06:34,841
Look at the gap between the rows.

116
00:06:34,841 --> 00:06:39,160
The jump from Mars to Jupiter is bigger than everything before it combined.

117
00:06:39,160 --> 00:06:44,170
The inner planets huddle close to the Sun, and then the solar system suddenly opens up.

118
00:06:44,170 --> 00:06:47,797
Neptune is about eighty times farther than Mars at its closest.

119
00:06:47,797 --> 00:06:55,341
If the Sun were a front door, Mars would sit about three hundred thirty meters from it, and Neptune six and a half kilometers away.

120
00:06:56,041 --> 00:06:59,173
So why can't we just point a rocket at Neptune and go?

121
00:06:59,173 --> 00:07:02,537
Because there is no such thing as standing still in space.

122
00:07:02,537 --> 00:07:07,524
You start out already moving around the Sun at thirty kilometers per second, sideways.

123
00:07:07,524 --> 00:07:13,150
Going outward means climbing out of the Sun's gravity, and every kilometer per second costs fuel.

124
00:07:13,850 --> 00:07:19,672
Parker Solar Probe got its record speed by falling toward the Sun, not by flying away from it.

125
00:07:19,672 --> 00:07:28,528
The fastest anything has ever been launched from Earth is New Horizons, on its way to Pluto, at about fifty eight thousand kilometers per hour.

126
00:07:28,528 --> 00:07:31,749
Even so, it took nine and a half years to get there.

127
00:07:32,449 --> 00:07:35,066
That's the real lesson of the solar system.

128
00:07:35,066 --> 00:07:38,839
The distances are huge, but the hard part is not the distance.

129
00:07:38,839 --> 00:07:44,011
It's the speed you have to gain, or lose, and the planets you have to borrow it from.

130
00:07:44,711 --> 00:07:47,263
So, by car, every planet is out of reach.

131
00:07:47,263 --> 00:07:50,561
By jet, only the nearest are reachable in a lifetime.

132
00:07:50,561 --> 00:07:56,038
By spacecraft, it's months to Mars, years to Jupiter, and more than a decade to Neptune.

133
00:07:56,038 --> 00:08:00,830
And Mercury, the closest planet on average, is one of the hardest to stop at.

134
